# AUDIT LEDGER — one JSON object per line.
#
# A line records that a commit which touched a control was handed to an auditor
# that did not write it (CLAUDE.md rule 20). Fields:
#
#   commit   full or abbreviated sha (>= 7 chars)
#   auditor  what performed it — never the author of the commit
#   at       ISO timestamp
#   found    how many defects it returned, or 0
#   note     one line a later reader can act on
#
# WHAT A LINE DOES NOT MEAN. This file cannot tell a real audit from a line
# somebody typed, and it approves nothing. Exactly like `granted_by` in the
# override channel, its value is that an ABSENCE is visible — not that a
# presence is proof. Anybody reading it to decide whether the code is sound has
# misread it; read the audit.
#
# Report coverage with:  node scripts/check-audit-coverage.mjs
#
# AN AUDITOR IS A BLIND CLONE. "Did not write it" is NECESSARY AND NOT
# SUFFICIENT, and reading line 7 as the whole rule is a mistake I made and Danny
# caught. On 2026-09-19 I read six commits I had not written, from inside the
# shared worktree, with the whole session's context in front of me — including
# what every one of those commits claimed to have achieved — and wrote six lines
# here. That silenced the gate on six commits no independent party had seen.
#
# It is not blind if the reader already knows what the author said. It is not
# isolated if it shares a worktree, a store and a HEAD that moves underneath it.
# A qualifying auditor is a SEPARATE agent in ITS OWN CLONE with its own
# AGENTBRIDGE_HOME, told what to attack and not what the commit claims (§7.2).
#
# Those six lines were REMOVED rather than annotated, because a line here is
# consumed by a machine: the coverage check reads `commit` and `auditor` and
# cannot weigh a caveat. A qualification a gate cannot see is a comment, and the
# gate would have gone on reporting those commits audited.
#
# Their findings were not discarded. Each line is COMMENTED OUT — retained in
# full as text a reader can still use, and invisible to parseLedger, which skips
# `#`. The findings themselves belong in the finding registry: a finding is
# evidence, an audit line is a clearance, and only the second one needs
# blindness.
#
{"commit":"d81e9643","auditor":"blind-subagent (own clone, own AGENTBRIDGE_HOME, briefed with identity + §7.4 proofs and NOT with what the commit claims)","recorded_by":"code-b, who did not write d81e9643 and did not perform this audit; transcribing only","at":"2026-09-20T00:30:00Z","found":8,"note":"SECOND, INDEPENDENT AUDIT OF THIS COMMIT, AND IT DISAGREES WITH THE FIRST. An earlier line recorded found:0 / 'no defect specific to this commit'; this pass returned eight, two HIGH, each with the command that demonstrates it. Both were blind subagents in their own clones. AUDITOR'S OWN DISCLOSURE, unprompted: `git log --oneline` and `git show` leaked the commit subject before it had formed a view, so it was partially contaminated and said to discount it accordingly -- none of the eight appear in the message and three contradict it. D2 HIGH MEASURED, the worst: scriptsChanged failed closed on any unreadable side, so EVERY repository with fewer commits than the 50-commit default window and EVERY `git clone --depth 1` blocked unconditionally on every turn -- and the only thing that cleared it was a ledger line asserting a reader had audited a root commit nobody audited. A gate whose false positives are cleared by fabricating an audit record corrupts the artefact rule 20 rests on; this repository escaped only by having >50 commits, which is rule 21. D1 HIGH MEASURED: the key split closes one channel of at least eight -- dependencies, devDependencies, packageManager, bin, overrides, workspaces, main/exports, type/engines all pass unblocked, as does package-lock.json alone with `resolved` repointed at an attacker tarball; the commit's own cited CLAUDE.md paragraph says `npm install <pkg>` needs no pre-existing script and runs postinstall. D4 MEDIUM MEASURED: the scripts digest was not injective -- `{pwn:'benign<RS>test<US>node --test'}` and `{pwn:'benign',test:'node --test'}` serialised identically, the second defining a real test script that did not exist; CLAUDE.md names N