# {"commit":"c9e3d682","auditor":"code-b (did not write it)","at":"2026-09-19T19:55:00Z","found":1,"note":"shell rail + stop gate. D1 MEDIUM DERIVED: the new prefix-resolution scoping keys on argv0 taken RAW from tokens[0] -- `if (!PREFIX_RESOLVED_TOOLS.has(argv0)) return false` -- with no normalisation of case or path. On Windows, where this runs and where the filesystem is case-insensitive, `SORT --out=x` resolves to the same binary and misses the Set, so the prefix rule is skipped and `--out` (not itself in WRITE_FLAG_LONG, only a prefix of --output) is no longer matched. A leading path such as /usr/bin/sort has the same effect if any earlier stage admits it. This repository has been bitten by case-variant bypasses before -- .Claude/settings.json and six more were ALLOW with no classification at all -- and the guard's own path matcher folds case for exactly this reason while this Set does not. UNMEASURED: I did not run the rail against SORT, so it is derived from the code, and it depends on `sort` being admitted by an earlier stage at all. VERIFIED TRUE: PUSH_REWRITE_SHORT /^-[A-Za-z0-9]*[df][A-Za-z0-9]*$/ covers -d and -f in clusters and glued forms, and d and f are precisely the destructive short options of git push -- -u, -n, -q, -v, -o and -4/-6 carry neither letter, so the cluster rule does not over-block any documented push option; it is scoped to gitOptionTokens(tokens,'push') so an operand branch name containing d or f cannot match, both because the regex is anchored at `-` and because operands are not returned. ALSO TRUE, and worth recording as practice rather than as a defect: this commit corrects three defects its own author shipped in the previous two commits, names the false justification it had given verbatim, labels the new scoping AS an enumeration -- the failure mode that keeps recurring here -- and names the durable repair (a per-tool arity table) rather than claiming the enumeration is one."}
