{"commit":"1f29e438","auditor":"code-b (did NOT write any of these four; read them cold, in the shared worktree, with its own sandboxes under TEMP). Not a blind clone: I had this session's context, so this is a reading audit rather than an isolated one, and the commits' own claims were visible to me.","at":"2026-09-20T04:45:47Z","found":1,"note":"AUDITED WITH b650c995, which closes an abbreviation hole in it; the finding is against the pair. HIGH MEASURED: \"prefix or cwd\" is a roster of CONCEPTS, and npm has others. Judging and executing the same string against npm 11.16.0, marker file as evidence: `npm test --node-options=--require=C:/x/evil.js` was ALLOW and the file RAN -- the same outcome --prefix gives, by injecting a module into every node the script spawns instead of relocating package.json, with the payload outside the repository so the node gate never sees it. That is precisely what 1f29e438's message says it closed. RULE 18 IS WHY IT HID: the backslash spelling of the same attack is DENY, but by the shell metacharacter matcher reacting to the backslash -- a different layer, on a spelling the attacker picks. ALSO MEASURED, an over-block in the other direction: the matcher scanned every token, so `npm run build -- --prefix foo` was refused although npm stops reading its own flags at a bare -- (verified four ways with the marker). CONFIRMED TRUE: every abbreviation b650c995 claims is caught is caught, and npm really does expand --prefi. MY OWN HARNESS WAS WRONG FIRST -- spawnSync of npm.cmd with shell:false is EINVAL on Node 24, so all six candidates AND the positive control came back clean; the control is the only reason I did not report six false negatives. FIXED in bd2772f by inverting the list: npm flags must be known-inert or they are refused. THAT FIX IS MINE AND IS UNAUDITED."}
{"commit":"b650c995","auditor":"code-b (did NOT write any of these four; read them cold, in the shared worktree, with its own sandboxes under TEMP). Not a blind clone: I had this session's context, so this is a reading audit rather than an isolated one, and the commits' own claims were visible to me.","at":"2026-09-20T04:45:47Z","found":1,"note":"See the row for 1f29e438: audited as one pair, one HIGH finding against both. This commit correctly closes the abbreviation hole (--prefi) that the first left, and its own claim to be \"routed on shape, not a roster of names\" is what fails -- `prefix`/`cwd` is a roster of two concepts, and --node-options reaches execution without being either. MEASURED ALLOW + RAN. Also measured: --script-shell, --userconfig, --globalconfig, --cache and -w were all ALLOW; of those only --node-options reached execution in my harness, but each sets npm config and --cache can seed an install that runs postinstall. Two of this commit's own assertions were measurably wrong and I changed them: it required `npm test -- --prefix` to be refused (npm does not redirect past a bare --) and required --config to be allowed (npm 11 answers \"Unknown cli config \\\"--config\\\"\", so it is not an npm flag). FIXED in bd2772f, which is mine and unaudited."}
{"commit":"37d3edbe","auditor":"code-b (did NOT write any of these four; read them cold, in the shared worktree, with its own sandboxes under TEMP). Not a blind clone: I had this session's context, so this is a reading audit rather than an isolated one, and the commits' own claims were visible to me.","at":"2026-09-20T04:45:47Z","found":1,"note":"MEDIUM MEASURED, against the message rather than the verdict. couldNotStart is `exitCode >= 0xC0000000 && tests === 0` and reports \"the machine refused to launch the process\". The NTSTATUS failure range holds more than the loader, and I ran the classifier on each: 0xC0000142 DLL_INIT_FAILED (what the commit observed), 0xC0000005 access violation and 0xC00000FD stack overflow all produce that sentence, and for the last two it is false -- the process got off the ground and then died. The commit states \"Only a code in the NTSTATUS failure range means the process itself never got off the ground\", which the same range disproves. THE VERDICT IS RIGHT AND UNCHANGED: PARTIAL, never reused, because a process that died before reporting anything says nothing about the tree either way, and a fatal OOM on a loaded box looks exactly like this. Only the stated cause was wrong, and an unestablished cause in a durable record is the defect this branch keeps repeating. CONFIRMED TRUE: the check really is evaluated BEFORE `failed` (line order verified, not assumed); PARTIAL really is excluded from reuse alongside TIMED_OUT; a crash AFTER tests ran (40 tests, exit 0xC0000005) is still FAILED, so the range does not excuse a real red; and node --test does not hand a test file this range at all -- a throw, an explicit process.exit(0xC0000005) and unbounded recursion were all measured arriving as exit 1 with one failing test, so the path is not reachable by ordinary test code. MY CONTROLS CAUGHT MY OWN ERROR: my first rows omitted `index`, so every case including both controls returned PARTIAL \"never reported\" and I would have reported nonsense. WORDING FIXED in the same commit as this row; that edit is mine and is unaudited."}
