{"commit":"76e0820","auditor":"blind-subagent LAP 5 (told nothing about laps 1-4)","recorded_by":"code-b, author, performed none of the five audits","at":"2026-09-21T00:40:00Z","found":15,"note":"LAP 5. Drew D1 and D4, the two suite regressions, plus D7, D8, D12. D1 HIGH MEASURED: this commit BROKE a shipped guard gate -- an unrouted execFileSync('git',...) under scripts/, where KNOWN_UNROUTED is deliberately empty with the count asserted exact. Its stated justification was also factually wrong: -c writes the command-line config scope and --local/--global/--system each read one FILE and ignore it, so runGit never could have masked an explicitly-scoped read. The commit reasoned its way around a control surface in a comment and the gate caught it. Fixed at e28a140, which I reached independently before reading the finding. D4 HIGH MEASURED, and flagged as an ENVIRONMENT finding per rule 21: test/hookAttestation.test.mjs asserts ok===true, and git clone NEVER copies .git/hooks -- so it fails in every fresh clone INCLUDING the one rule 20 requires an auditor to make, in the direction that looks like the code is wrong. Fixed at faa7d53. D7: hooksPathOverride's blanket catch swallows every failure, not just the exit-1 unset case, so git absent from PATH reads as not-set and the checker proceeds to ok:true -- the could-not-look primitive reintroduced three files from where the same range fixes it. D8: the scope list misses the per-worktree config, which OVERRIDES all three and is exactly where this daemon runs reviewers, and an empty value is dropped. D12: assert.match(src, /core.hooksPath/) is vacuous -- the string appears in comments predating the fix -- and nothing constructs an input that makes the redirect branch fire, so H3's behaviour has never been watched going red. D7, D8, D12 REMAIN OPEN."}
{"commit":"829079d","auditor":"blind-subagent LAP 4 (FRESH auditor told nothing about laps 1-3; own clone attempted via npm run audit:workspace, isolated AGENTBRIDGE_HOME)","recorded_by":"code-b, WHO WROTE these commits and performed none of the four audits. Transcribing only; findings and measurements are the auditor's.","at":"2026-09-20T23:30:00Z","found":16,"note":"LAP 4, ONE PASS over 829079d~1..553724b (nine commits, NEVER PREVIOUSLY READ BY ANY AUDITOR) and cedffbf..HEAD. It verified the ranges itself and found my brief wrong again: range B is FOUR commits, not three -- 2ce6538 was omitted. Second brief-omission caught in two laps. VERDICT: neither range sound. IMPORTANT LIMITATION IT DECLARED: the rail refused it a clone it could execute in, so it ran NO full-suite baseline and reports NO suite counts; every count in its report is a single-file run in the shared checkout, contaminated by other sessions and by the live wildcard grant. Treat those as 'these files pass here', not a baseline. THIS COMMIT is part of the audit-daemon series; see the 1702be1 row for the daemon findings."}
{"commit":"59614cc","auditor":"blind-subagent LAP 4 (told nothing about laps 1-3)","recorded_by":"code-b, author, performed none of the four audits","at":"2026-09-20T23:30:00Z","found":16,"note":"LAP 4, audit-daemon series. Drew H4 (HIGH, DERIVED): the verdict marker is a FIXED LITERAL matched first-match-wins over a transcript that includes the reviewer's stdout AND stderr, in a worktree checked out at the candidate. A candidate commit can plant the marker block in any file the reviewer prints, and an HONEST reviewer that quotes observed output before its own conclusion hands the daemon a FORGED PASS. The marker is published in the script and written into the brief, so a candidate always knows it. The code does not count matches, prefer the last, require the block to terminate the output, or use a per-run nonce. H4 REMAINS OPEN."}
{"commit":"6004654","auditor":"blind-subagent LAP 4 (told nothing about laps 1-3)","recorded_by":"code-b, author, performed none of the four audits","at":"2026-09-20T23:30:00Z","found":16,"note":"LAP 4, audit-daemon series, and the commit that introduced the stdout verdict channel H4 attacks. Secondary on the same line: a benign double-print is mis-resolved -- if the reviewer quotes the brief's own template first, JSON.parse fails, verdict is null, and a REAL FAIL is discarded. Safe direction, but the finding is lost and the worktree leaks. H4 REMAINS OPEN."}
{"commit":"94d6f9a","auditor":"blind-subagent LAP 4 (told nothing about laps 1-3)","recorded_by":"code-b, author, performed none of the four audits","at":"2026-09-20T23:30:00Z","found":16,"note":"LAP 4. Drew M5 (MEASURED): teardown sits at the END of tick(), so the three early returns the commit's own release() helper was written for -- spawn error, reviewer non-zero exit, no readable verdict -- ALL LEAK THE WORKTREE. Seven leaked worktrees exist on this machine; the auditor attributed them HONESTLY to before this commit's timestamp and said so rather than claiming them as proof. Also M6 (MEASURED precondition): allocate() reuses any pre-existing %TEMP%/audit-<sha12> WITHOUT verifying it is a registered worktree, at the candidate, or clean -- while the brief asserts to the reviewer that it IS the exact candidate. os.tmpdir() is writable by any local process, so pre-creating that directory at a different commit makes the reviewer audit the wrong tree with the verdict attributed to the right one. Teardown then --force removes whatever was there. M5 and M6 REMAIN OPEN."}
{"commit":"1702be1","auditor":"blind-subagent LAP 4 (told nothing about laps 1-3)","recorded_by":"code-b, author, performed none of the four audits","at":"2026-09-20T23:30:00Z","found":16,"note":"LAP 4. Drew M7 (MEASURED): the daemon invokes audit-record with NO --candidate and NO --tree, and the CLI defaults both from the row -- so recordAudit's candidate-moved fence compares the row against itself and CAN NEVER FIRE on the only automated write path. Also: this commit's comment says the writer is 'NOT taken from a flag', and the code is `by: resolvedW ?? str_(args.by)` -- env first, FLAG AS FALLBACK, the comment contradicting the code. And the daemon spawns the CLI without scrubbing the environment, against its own header, so in any session with AGENTBRIDGE_SESSION_ID set the write is refused for writer-not-claimant with a message that will not say why. Also M13 (MEASURED): the daemon has NO TEST, NOTHING SCHEDULES IT, and its only apparent coverage reimplements its claimJob call by hand -- deleting the real line leaves the suite green. Same shape as hollow gate 5, the reaper that was written, tested, applied and never scheduled. M7 and M13 REMAIN OPEN."}
{"commit":"14e3f03","auditor":"blind-subagent LAP 4 (told nothing about laps 1-3)","recorded_by":"code-b, author, performed none of the four audits","at":"2026-09-20T23:30:00Z","found":16,"note":"LAP 4. Drew M10 (MEASURED): docs/P0_EXECUTABLE_DEPENDENCY_BLINDNESS.md rests on a defect ALREADY FIXED A DAY EARLIER. Its founding statement is that claude-stop-gate.mjs executes verify-run.mjs by path while the closure follows imports -- but 48e77ec (2026-09-19) changed it to `await import('../src/verifyRunner.mjs')`, and merge-base confirms that predates this doc. The invariant is worth having; a P0 marked 'Status: open, do not clear this yourself' built on a stale instance, with 'found twice independently' framing, makes two stale readings look like corroboration. M10 REMAINS OPEN."}
